(Title) Tj
T*
(Multimodal Nonpharmacologic Delirium Prevention Reduces Postoperative Delirium in Older) Tj
T*
(Adults: A Stepped-Wedge Trial) Tj
T*
() Tj
T*
(Abstract) Tj
T*
(Postoperative delirium in older surgical patients remains a significant quality and safety) Tj
T*
(challenge despite available prevention strategies. We evaluated a comprehensive, team-) Tj
T*
(based delirium prevention program implemented across 5 hospitals in 1470 patients aged 70) Tj
T*
(or older undergoing elective orthopedic, general, or cardiac surgery. The multimodal) Tj
T*
(program included staff education, patient assessment of delirium risk factors, and) Tj
T*
(individualized non-pharmacologic interventions \(cognitive engagement, sensory) Tj
T*
(optimization, mobility promotion, sleep support\). Overall, the intervention reduced) Tj
T*
(postoperative delirium incidence by approximately 13% compared to standard care, with) Tj
T*
(greater absolute reductions among patients undergoing orthopedic or general surgery) Tj
T*
(\(effect most pronounced for these procedure types\). Delirium duration--days spent delirious) Tj
T*
(during hospitalization--was also significantly reduced in the intervention group. The) Tj
T*
(prevention program did not produce significant delirium reduction in cardiac surgery) Tj
T*
(patients. No serious adverse events related to the prevention protocol were documented.) Tj
T*
(Implementation was feasible across diverse hospital settings with appropriate staff) Tj
T*
(training. These findings support adoption of multimodal delirium prevention programs,) Tj
T*
(particularly for older patients undergoing orthopedic and general surgical procedures.) Tj
